WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.030
A student's search runs in a

2
00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.770
blink on ten thousand records.

3
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:05.930
On the university's ten

4
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.810
million, it never finishes.

5
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.610
Their code was correct.

6
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.040
What did they miss?

7
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.680
Today: what Big-O really says, why

8
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.360
students confuse it with speed, and how

9
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.220
to teach growth rates so they stick.

10
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.540
Big-O describes how running time

11
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.300
grows as the input grows.

12
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.380
It ignores constant

13
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.380
factors and small inputs.

14
00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:33.350
It is an upper bound, while Theta gives a

15
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.170
tight bound and Omega a lower one.

16
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.810
We write f of n is O of g of n when some

17
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:46.670
constant c and some n zero exist with f at

18
00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.290
most c times g for every n beyond n zero.

19
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.600
The definition is about the

20
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.340
eventual shape of the curve.

21
00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.040
A loop inside a loop over n

22
00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.460
items does n squared steps.

23
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.950
Halving the range each time, as binary

24
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.990
search does, takes about log n steps.

25
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:12.090
At ten million records, that is a hundred

26
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.330
trillion steps against about twenty four.

27
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.550
Divide-and-conquer algorithms give

28
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.030
recurrences such as T of n equals

29
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.990
two T of n over two plus n.

30
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.840
The master theorem solves

31
00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:26.760
the common forms.

32
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.910
Merge sort comes out at n log n.

33
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.480
Students often think Big-O means

34
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.540
seconds, or that O of n squared is

35
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.280
always slower than O of n.

36
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.310
Let them time two algorithms on

37
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.410
growing inputs and plot the curves.

38
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.280
Then introduce the definition

39
00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:47.860
to explain what they saw.

40
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:49.580
Quick check!

41
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.120
One algorithm takes one hundred n

42
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.900
steps, another takes n squared.

43
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.120
For large n, which is slower?

44
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.690
The n squared one, once n

45
00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:03.730
passes one hundred.

46
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.700
Below that, it is actually faster.

47
00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.530
Big-O tells you who wins in the end,

48
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.730
not who wins on small inputs.

49
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.470
In the app, open the Big-O lab.

50
00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.590
Grow the input and watch the step counts

51
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.370
of different algorithms pull apart.

52
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.280
Your turn.

53
00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.470
A student says linear

54
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.710
search is O of n squared.

55
00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.750
Is that statement true?

56
00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.740
True, but not tight: O is only an upper

57
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.420
bound, and Theta of n is the precise

58
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:37.960
answer.

59
00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.900
Remember: Big-O is about growth,

60
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.720
constants vanish, and recurrences

61
00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.340
solve divide and conquer.

62
00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.320
Try it in your next lecture: give pairs

63
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.820
insertion sort and merge sort, and

64
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.020
inputs from ten to ten thousand.

65
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:57.910
They time both and plot the results.

66
00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:02.530
Then ask which curve matches n log n.
